In this lesson, we'll look at the unit circle and how we measure angles using radians, the natural language of circular motion. Picture a circle of radius one, sitting right at the origin of a graph. Now imagine a number line wrapping around it, starting at the point on the right where the value is zero, and winding anticlockwise as the numbers grow. Every real number lands on exactly one spot on the circle, which is a really elegant way to link the straight number line to a circle. So what is a radian? It's simply the angle you get when an arc of length one is drawn on that unit circle. That means one full trip all the way around is two pi radians, because the full circumference of a unit circle is two pi. From there, the arc length on any circle of radius r is just r times the angle in radians, which is why radians feel so natural for anything spinning in a circle. And converting between degrees and radians is easy: multiply degrees by pi over one hundred eighty to switch to radians, and flip it the other way by multiplying radians by one hundred eighty over pi. Now let's see it in action.